00:14:48.501 --> 00:14:52.108
I really wanted to get back to you and
see how you were doing.

00:14:52.108 --> 00:14:53.438
Well, I'm doing fine.

00:14:53.438 --> 00:14:55.150
[LAUGH] How are you doing?

00:14:55.150 --> 00:14:56.875
Well, I you know, I'm getting work out.

00:14:56.875 --> 00:14:57.840
I was looking for a job.

00:14:57.840 --> 00:15:00.700
Well what happened with the last
thing that you called me about two

00:15:00.700 --> 00:15:03.820
years ago that you never came back and
told me what happened about that.

00:15:03.820 --> 00:15:08.817
And, maybe I'll be polite to him,
maybe I want more likely they don't

00:15:08.817 --> 00:15:13.162
somehow I'm out of town or
something when that call comes in.

00:15:13.162 --> 00:15:16.090
And my EA just handles it,
it never sees the light of day.

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:19.896
Stay connected to the people
who have helped you.

00:15:23.249 --> 00:15:28.742
And my career was the executive search
business, I started very early.

00:15:28.742 --> 00:15:32.124
I think it was like 32, I spent 30
years with Hedrick and struggles.

00:15:32.124 --> 00:15:35.793
We were the largest search from the world
for a long time, I think pretty

00:15:35.793 --> 00:15:39.904
much the most respected senior executive
search firm in the world then and now.

00:15:39.904 --> 00:15:43.822
I did a lot of things with the firm
including spending the last five years

00:15:43.822 --> 00:15:46.708
before I retired from that as,
as chairman and CEO.

00:15:46.708 --> 00:15:51.379
We were a public company by then with
70 offices in 40 countries and 500 and

00:15:51.379 --> 00:15:54.810
some partners spread
out all over the place.

00:15:54.810 --> 00:16:01.314
And, It's amazing to me that so
many people who interact with

00:16:01.314 --> 00:16:06.896
executive search firms Don't have hardly
any clue about how the profession works,

00:16:06.896 --> 00:16:10.454
and what it does, and what it doesn't do,
and how it can work for you.

00:16:10.454 --> 00:16:13.338
Let me give you an example.

00:16:13.338 --> 00:16:17.037
There's thousands of
search firm partners and

00:16:17.037 --> 00:16:22.220
my firm had about 500,
they were spread out all over the world.

00:16:22.220 --> 00:16:27.528
But for any one particular thing,
particularly at the very senior

00:16:27.528 --> 00:16:34.277
level where recruiters in every firm begin
to specialize on industry and function.

00:16:34.277 --> 00:16:39.935
For somebody in this audience, there
might have been in my firm 3, 4, maybe 5

00:16:39.935 --> 00:16:45.452
of the 500 recruiters that it would be
useful to build a relationship with.

00:16:45.452 --> 00:16:50.026
And 495 who might like you,
might be friends, might be a neighbor,

00:16:50.026 --> 00:16:54.066
might help you in a different way,
I'll come back to that, but

00:16:54.066 --> 00:16:59.910
would never be in a position to have an
assignment where you could be a candidate.

00:16:59.910 --> 00:17:04.715
Because maybe you're consumer
products chief marketing officer,

00:17:04.715 --> 00:17:09.597
and your next door neighbor is a partner
in one of the big five executive

00:17:09.597 --> 00:17:14.401
recruiting firms, and does chief
information officer searches for

00:17:14.401 --> 00:17:19.240
industrial companies, pretty much only and
dominates that space.

00:17:19.240 --> 00:17:24.168
There is zero chance that that person
will ever have a direct assignment that

00:17:24.168 --> 00:17:26.480
you can be a candidate for.

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:31.410
But that person knows those five
people who are the five people in that

00:17:31.410 --> 00:17:33.888
firm that you ought to get to know.

00:17:33.888 --> 00:17:38.149
And the way to use them if you can get
a relationship with them is tell them

00:17:38.149 --> 00:17:43.119
exactly either what kind of person or if
you can do your research, it's usually not

00:17:43.119 --> 00:17:47.965
very hard, what specific person in their
firm you'd like to get introduced to.

00:17:47.965 --> 00:17:51.940
And they'll probably do that for
you because it's the stock in trade.

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:56.765
Recruiters work for clients,
we work exclusively for clients,

00:17:56.765 --> 00:18:01.691
but we have this kind of odd
three-party symbiotic relationship.

00:18:01.691 --> 00:18:06.999
It's completely unique in professional
service representation where

00:18:06.999 --> 00:18:12.393
we get paid by one side of a transaction,
but have to be perceived as fair,

00:18:12.393 --> 00:18:15.972
balanced, and
delivering value to both sides.

00:18:15.972 --> 00:18:17.136
Lawyers don't get that.

00:18:17.136 --> 00:18:19.712
I mean, you got two companies
fighting with each other,

00:18:19.712 --> 00:18:22.189
company a has got a lawyer,
company b has got a lawyer.

00:18:22.189 --> 00:18:25.048
The lawyers fight with each other, but
they know who they're representing.

00:18:25.048 --> 00:18:27.230
They're not trying to be fair,
they're advocates.

00:18:27.230 --> 00:18:29.365
They're advocates for their side.

00:18:29.365 --> 00:18:32.969
Marketing people, PR people,
accountants, they're working for

00:18:32.969 --> 00:18:35.526
their clients and
every client has one of those.

00:18:35.526 --> 00:18:40.857
But the search profession is pretty odd,
so the people that do it have to tread

00:18:40.857 --> 00:18:46.697
this line between loyalty to a client and
respect and loyalty among the candidates.

00:18:46.697 --> 00:18:51.216
because unless that they can put
a transaction together with two willing

00:18:51.216 --> 00:18:53.198
parties who are both smart and

00:18:53.198 --> 00:18:59.340
ideally not going to do something that's
not in the both of their best interests.

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:02.815
No transaction happens, no search gets
completed, and nothing gets done,

00:19:02.815 --> 00:19:06.140
and that person doesn't stay
in the business very long.

00:19:06.140 --> 00:19:11.212
So you can make that work for
you as long as you understand that

00:19:11.212 --> 00:19:16.980
it's not the recruiter's job to
find you your next position.

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:22.820
It's the recruiter's job to solve
the problem that their client has.

00:19:22.820 --> 00:19:27.722
And if you can be the solution to that
problem, then they're going to be real

00:19:27.722 --> 00:19:32.484
happy to bring you forward and
have you be a candidate for that project.

00:19:32.484 --> 00:19:36.473
If you're not the solution to that
problem, two things can happen.

00:19:36.473 --> 00:19:41.337
If you recognize it early on and raise
your hand and say this isn't right for me,

00:19:41.337 --> 00:19:44.400
what I'm really interested in is x.

00:19:44.400 --> 00:19:47.323
Let me try to help you with a suggestion
of somebody better than me for

00:19:47.323 --> 00:19:48.400
this project.

00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:50.072
And would you remember me for something or

00:19:50.072 --> 00:19:53.007
introduce me to one of your colleagues
who's working on what I want to do?

00:19:53.007 --> 00:19:55.738
That's a good interaction,
that will serve you well.

00:19:55.738 --> 00:19:59.848
What won't serve you well is either trying
to convince a recruiter, any recruiter,

00:19:59.848 --> 00:20:00.875
really at any level,

00:20:00.875 --> 00:20:04.773
that you're something that you're not
because they'll figure it out eventually.

00:20:04.773 --> 00:20:08.358
Or that you are interested in something
that in the end you're really not

00:20:08.358 --> 00:20:11.712
interested in because you're
wasting the recruiter's time which

00:20:11.712 --> 00:20:14.119
is the only asset they have
to make a living with,

00:20:14.119 --> 00:20:17.032
and they don't appreciate it
when their time's wasted.

00:20:17.032 --> 00:20:22.680
They know every set of interactions is not
going to lead to a successful outcome.

00:20:22.680 --> 00:20:25.560
And you may go all the way to the end and
you may decide or

00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:28.904
the client may decide this isn't right,
it didn't happen.

00:20:28.904 --> 00:20:32.496
But as long as that was done with good
faith, nobody has any hard feelings.

00:20:32.496 --> 00:20:36.150
But if it was done with bad faith, and
bad faith can be on the client side too,

00:20:36.150 --> 00:20:37.766
I've experienced this as well.

00:20:37.766 --> 00:20:40.984
In which case I apologized
to the candidates and

00:20:40.984 --> 00:20:43.410
try to make it right if I can.

00:20:43.410 --> 00:20:49.197
But nobody worries about
that as long as everybody

00:20:49.197 --> 00:20:54.587
is proceeding along
the path with good faith.

00:20:54.587 --> 00:20:56.117
Be specific, be special,

00:20:56.117 --> 00:21:00.047
try to be very clear when you're
dealing with recruiters of any kind.

00:21:00.047 --> 00:21:01.552
What is it you want?

00:21:01.552 --> 00:21:03.109
What you're willing to do?

00:21:03.109 --> 00:21:05.594
What you're not willing to do?

00:21:05.594 --> 00:21:10.825
And ask the kind of questions to try to
decide if this opportunity that they've

00:21:10.825 --> 00:21:15.671
put in front of you or called you about
is the right potential one for you.

00:21:15.671 --> 00:21:16.832
If it is, say so.

00:21:16.832 --> 00:21:20.143
Don't be cute,
don't play hard to get, be honest,

00:21:20.143 --> 00:21:23.688
there would be plenty of time for
negotiation at the end.

00:21:23.688 --> 00:21:28.944
If it isn't, pull out fast and
don't waste your time.

00:21:28.944 --> 00:21:31.851
Don't lie or embellish your resume.

00:21:31.851 --> 00:21:36.612
My colleagues and
I spent years honing skills to

00:21:36.612 --> 00:21:40.189
smell bullshit coming a mile away.

00:21:40.189 --> 00:21:44.946
I mean, a good recruiter will not let
you get away with claiming something

00:21:44.946 --> 00:21:49.860
that isn't true, that's fluffy,
credit for something that really maybe

00:21:49.860 --> 00:21:54.336
you had a small part of and
you're trying to take too much credit for.

00:21:54.336 --> 00:21:58.550
You don't need to do that,
you've got good records.

00:21:58.550 --> 00:22:03.300
Just be honest and
try to position yourself someplace where

00:22:03.300 --> 00:22:08.240
your skill set and
your interests fit what the clients needs.

00:22:09.900 --> 00:22:10.995
Covered some of this other stuff.

00:22:10.995 --> 00:22:14.429
The second one though I
want to come back to because

00:22:14.429 --> 00:22:19.367
as you think about what it is that you
want to do, there is a role there for

00:22:19.367 --> 00:22:24.315
executive recruiters to play over
a long period in your career.

00:22:24.315 --> 00:22:29.661
And the key, I think for all of you, is
to figure out literally that handful and

00:22:29.661 --> 00:22:33.958
it's probably no more than that
in any particular geography.

00:22:33.958 --> 00:22:37.750
If you go more broadly global,
it could be a little more,

00:22:37.750 --> 00:22:40.420
it's still not a big number.

00:22:40.420 --> 00:22:45.405
And I doubt for most of you,
the critical mass of recruiters

00:22:45.405 --> 00:22:50.091
among the major search firms
who would be in a position to

00:22:50.091 --> 00:22:54.876
be really good career-long allies and
sources for you,

00:22:54.876 --> 00:23:01.180
I would be very surprised if that was
more than 10 or 12 people in total.

00:23:02.490 --> 00:23:06.188
That's a number of people that
you can over time get to know.

00:23:06.188 --> 00:23:10.298
It's not hundreds, that's hard, but
10, 6, 8, 10, 12, you can do that.

00:23:10.298 --> 00:23:15.160
Particularly, if you identify who they are
and have a strategy to get to know them in

00:23:15.160 --> 00:23:19.398
a positive way through introductions
of people, other search people,

00:23:19.398 --> 00:23:20.941
others who will help you.

00:23:20.941 --> 00:23:25.290
But get to know them in a positive way,
stay connected, don't be a pain in

00:23:25.290 --> 00:23:30.357
the neck, but stay connected, and build
a very long-term relationship with them.

00:23:30.357 --> 00:23:34.481
Particularly the ones that are working
in your chosen field a level or

00:23:34.481 --> 00:23:36.198
two above where you are now.

00:23:36.198 --> 00:23:39.526
Because over time, they'll move up too,
and as you move up,

00:23:39.526 --> 00:23:42.664
you'll be heading into their
space from below as it will.

00:23:42.664 --> 00:23:45.491
And those are the people you
want to be dealing with,

00:23:45.491 --> 00:23:49.894
not the ones that are working at or below
your level now, which by the time you get

00:23:49.894 --> 00:23:54.790
your next promotion, you've outgrown
them and they can't help you anymore.

00:23:54.790 --> 00:23:57.553
So you want to be aiming high.

00:23:57.553 --> 00:24:03.620
So what do companies want
when they're hiring people?

00:24:03.620 --> 00:24:08.333
Companies in every cycle are hiring
people, they're trying to hire good

00:24:08.333 --> 00:24:13.210
people they want to understand what your
career is and how it fits with them.

00:24:15.240 --> 00:24:20.016
They're looking for skills and experience
that closely match their needs as they see

00:24:20.016 --> 00:24:24.670
them and probably you can't do a whole lot
to convince them to see it some other way.

00:24:25.710 --> 00:24:30.440
This third one's really
important because anybody,

00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:36.900
even the CEO, hiring somebody has
got to answer to his or her board.

00:24:36.900 --> 00:24:41.933
And everybody else has a boss, maybe
more than one boss, and if they're going

00:24:41.933 --> 00:24:46.690
to hire you go explain to somebody
else probably somebody you never met.

00:24:46.690 --> 00:24:47.671
Maybe you met him but

00:24:47.671 --> 00:24:51.650
maybe you didn't, why they want to
hire you as opposed to somebody else.

00:24:51.650 --> 00:24:54.626
And it's your job if you want
that job to make it easy for

00:24:54.626 --> 00:24:58.510
them to explain to whoever they have
to explain to why it is that they want

00:24:58.510 --> 00:25:02.430
to hire you as opposed to anybody
else that they might hire.

00:25:02.430 --> 00:25:06.421
So if you put yourself in that mindset and
say, you got to tell a story, not only

00:25:06.421 --> 00:25:10.476
will they believe but that they can
retell, because they're going to have to,

00:25:10.476 --> 00:25:14.180
they're going to have to justify
this decision to somebody.

00:25:14.180 --> 00:25:17.183
And so,
the easier you can make that for them,

00:25:17.183 --> 00:25:21.341
the more likely they are to hire you,
which says personality and

00:25:21.341 --> 00:25:25.960
style that fits their culture,
every company has has a culture.

00:25:25.960 --> 00:25:31.260
Some cultures are more comfortable for
some people than others some companies

00:25:31.260 --> 00:25:37.070
are more female friendly than others,
some are more ethnically open than others.

00:25:37.070 --> 00:25:41.228
Some are more open to different
nationalities than others you can fight

00:25:41.228 --> 00:25:44.623
that We can all deplore that and
say that's horrible, but

00:25:44.623 --> 00:25:46.910
you're looking for a job.

00:25:46.910 --> 00:25:52.030
And I mean if you if you go back to one
of the things that I've always loved that

00:25:52.030 --> 00:25:57.230
my good friend Jim Collins, Jim probably
speaks around here once in a while,

00:25:57.230 --> 00:26:00.270
is fond of saying is is look for
the open door.

00:26:01.380 --> 00:26:06.061
He said so many people go through their
lives spending an awful lot of energy,

00:26:06.061 --> 00:26:10.930
trying to pound their way through
doors that are closed and locked.

00:26:10.930 --> 00:26:14.892
It's possible, but
it's hard down the hall, three doors down,

00:26:14.892 --> 00:26:20.200
maybe there's an open door with somebody
there that would say, come on in.

00:26:20.200 --> 00:26:24.070
Once in a while you may want to think
about maybe I'll go down there and

00:26:24.070 --> 00:26:27.804
go through that open door as opposed
to trying to find my way through

00:26:27.804 --> 00:26:31.336
this one it's locked or locked to
me now it may be locked to me for

00:26:31.336 --> 00:26:33.428
reasons that I find reprehensible.

00:26:35.541 --> 00:26:39.921
Yeah, you can decide if you want to
try to change that good luck to you or

00:26:39.921 --> 00:26:43.707
you can say, I'll do that in my
spare time, but I need a job so

00:26:43.707 --> 00:26:48.710
I want to go someplace where somebody
wants me for what I am for who I am.

00:26:48.710 --> 00:26:53.606
And they like what I am as opposed
to me trying to convince them they

00:26:53.606 --> 00:26:58.679
should like what I am when they don't
simple as that no side issues or

00:26:58.679 --> 00:27:02.970
hassles, the more the easier
you make it to recruit you.

00:27:04.770 --> 00:27:08.772
The more likely it is that somebody is
going to do that because this gets back

00:27:08.772 --> 00:27:12.776
again to they going to explain this to
somebody else if they want somebody to

00:27:12.776 --> 00:27:16.904
start the first of next month, and
you don't want to start for three months,

00:27:16.904 --> 00:27:21.669
that's a problem for them, particularly if
they've got another candidate might not be

00:27:21.669 --> 00:27:25.000
as good as us willing to
start when they want.

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:28.697
If you need a lot more compensation
than they're prepared to pay or

00:27:28.697 --> 00:27:32.262
you have a whole lot of other
complicated things that they have to

00:27:32.262 --> 00:27:36.570
deal with that's hard, so,
the point there is think about what it is.

00:27:36.570 --> 00:27:41.024
Somebody said the difference between what
you'd like to have and what you need

00:27:41.024 --> 00:27:46.590
to have focus on that make it easy to hire
you, compensation demands they can meet.

00:27:46.590 --> 00:27:50.753
Sometimes you can push the boundaries
We've got another little

00:27:50.753 --> 00:27:54.000
Presentation I do on
negotiating compensation.

00:27:54.000 --> 00:27:56.520
I don't think we're going to have
the time to do that tonight, but

00:27:56.520 --> 00:28:00.130
if anybody has any questions about that,
we'll handle it in the Q and A.

00:28:00.130 --> 00:28:03.984
And once somebody hires you, they want
you to go, they want you to onboard,

00:28:03.984 --> 00:28:07.793
you should want that too, but they want
you to onboard starting and engaged

00:28:10.382 --> 00:28:15.022
So we're in Silicon Valley, and many of
you either have work in startups in fact,

00:28:15.022 --> 00:28:17.460
how many of you have worked in start-ups?

00:28:18.860 --> 00:28:20.826
Not too many usually it's more okay,

00:28:20.826 --> 00:28:24.570
how many of you are thinking
about working in startups?

00:28:24.570 --> 00:28:28.990
Okay, I mean, let me know if I got the
maths, right how many of you have worked

00:28:28.990 --> 00:28:33.930
in startups before or thinking about
going back into startups again?

00:28:33.930 --> 00:28:38.683
One, two three, okay, good so
you didn't get turned off

00:28:38.683 --> 00:28:43.350
by the first time somebody
asked I'm not sure who it was.

00:28:43.350 --> 00:28:47.604
It might have been John Doerr at
Kleiner Perkins said, if one time he said,

00:28:47.604 --> 00:28:51.048
if you're trying to figure out
if you're an entrepreneur,

00:28:51.048 --> 00:28:55.120
you're not which I always
thought was kind of interesting.

00:28:55.120 --> 00:28:57.040
If you need structure,

00:28:57.040 --> 00:29:02.224
then nothing wrong with that big
companies need a lot of people,

00:29:02.224 --> 00:29:07.620
the good places to work, and
if that's you, that's fine go big.

00:29:07.620 --> 00:29:12.206
Lots of big companies recruiting here lots
of big companies are great places to work

00:29:12.206 --> 00:29:16.200
for entire careers if you decide to go
to a start-up, I would pose not for

00:29:16.200 --> 00:29:19.960
everybody but for the people in
this room because you're special.

00:29:20.970 --> 00:29:25.682
You're a special group of people
I would say if it's a start-up,

00:29:25.682 --> 00:29:29.381
you want to be focused on
a couple of things and I say,

00:29:29.381 --> 00:29:34.510
will you be at the table at the table
means are you going to be in the group,

00:29:34.510 --> 00:29:39.060
the small group of people
that makes the key decisions.

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:42.577
They're in a closed room with the door
closed when something big is coming down

00:29:42.577 --> 00:29:46.230
and bet the company are really
important decisions have to be made.

00:29:46.230 --> 00:29:50.424
If you're at that table, doesn't matter
a whole lot what your job title is,

00:29:50.424 --> 00:29:54.875
what your compensation is, really are or
any else because it'll all be taken care

00:29:54.875 --> 00:29:57.798
of if you're not at the table,
and you're outside.

00:29:57.798 --> 00:30:02.197
Looking through the keyhole trying to
figure out what's going on in that room

00:30:02.197 --> 00:30:06.525
when the key decisions are being made,
I would say to you if to start up most of

00:30:06.525 --> 00:30:09.589
the time, the risk reward ratio for
you is not right.

00:30:11.360 --> 00:30:15.896
That puts you in in the world of
four box matrix is in the high risk,

00:30:15.896 --> 00:30:17.132
low reward box and

00:30:17.132 --> 00:30:23.620
I don't think that's a box that you ought
to be intentionally aiming yourself to.

00:30:23.620 --> 00:30:29.105
That if the company is wildly successful,
you make a little bit of money and

00:30:29.105 --> 00:30:34.066
you weren't really part of the team
that drove it on the other hand,

00:30:34.066 --> 00:30:39.400
if you're in the high risk,
high reward box, yeah, that can be great.

00:30:41.600 --> 00:30:42.500
Can you handle the risk?

00:30:44.620 --> 00:30:45.806
What do you do if it fails?

00:30:45.806 --> 00:30:51.921
Some cultures, some geographies failures
really hard it's really hard to explain or

00:30:51.921 --> 00:30:55.353
somebody said once how
are you going to go home and

00:30:55.353 --> 00:30:58.470
tell your mother that your company failed.

00:30:58.470 --> 00:31:00.930
If you grew up around
Silicon Valley it was okay,

00:31:00.930 --> 00:31:03.390
big deal what are you going to do next?

00:31:03.390 --> 00:31:05.674
But there's some places in the country and

00:31:05.674 --> 00:31:09.010
in the world where that's going
to be really hard for you?

00:31:09.010 --> 00:31:12.112
And if it is, and
you don't feel like you're up for it,

00:31:12.112 --> 00:31:14.290
maybe that's not where you want to go.

00:31:15.340 --> 00:31:17.910
Are the leaders of the startup
professionals or are they friends?

00:31:19.080 --> 00:31:23.874
I put up a big red flag every time I see a
startup that's three college roommates and

00:31:23.874 --> 00:31:27.310
get together because they
want to start a company.

00:31:27.310 --> 00:31:30.864
I'll be the CEO and so you'd be the
marketing VP and what do you want to be

00:31:30.864 --> 00:31:35.720
okay, engineering well, we got to have one
okay, well, you're really not very good.

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:39.127
That's a bad way to get
a company started and

00:31:39.127 --> 00:31:43.639
if you get into a company where
the officers are friends and

00:31:43.639 --> 00:31:50.800
they wouldn't be picked for their talent
in a fair fight against other people.

00:31:50.800 --> 00:31:55.629
This has a very high probability of coming
out with the worst possible outcome

00:31:55.629 --> 00:32:00.158
you could imagine, which is you lose
the company, you lose the job, and

00:32:00.158 --> 00:32:03.220
you probably lose your
friends in the process.

00:32:03.220 --> 00:32:08.053
So I just say Massive caveats
on going in any startup where

00:32:08.053 --> 00:32:12.063
a bunch of your friends get together and
choose up sides.

00:32:12.063 --> 00:32:15.758
Is the financing solid for
at least a year?

00:32:15.758 --> 00:32:20.648
Because you don't want to be coming
into something unless there's at

00:32:20.648 --> 00:32:25.538
least a year of runway there before
the company needs to raise money,

00:32:25.538 --> 00:32:28.840
because maybe it won't, and that's tough.

00:32:28.840 --> 00:32:32.075
As I said, if it's a tough choice, say no.

00:32:32.075 --> 00:32:34.295
We have like two slides left and
we're going to Q&amp;A.

00:32:34.295 --> 00:32:36.699
I know you had do you have a question
you want to ask before you left?

00:32:36.699 --> 00:32:41.574
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] Contrary to somebody,

00:32:41.574 --> 00:32:43.870
I recruited CEOs mostly for a living, and

00:32:43.870 --> 00:32:48.264
there's a popular wisdom that you really
want to go out when you're looking for

00:32:48.264 --> 00:32:52.546
a CEO, and find somebody that's done
this successfully two or three times.

00:32:52.546 --> 00:32:56.844
And that's the spiel we gave to our
clients, it was a big part of our sale.

00:32:56.844 --> 00:33:00.959
They bought it most of the time,
wasn't true.

00:33:00.959 --> 00:33:06.653
The fact of the matter is, most successful
CEOs that you could think about and

00:33:06.653 --> 00:33:09.382
that you could identify did the job.

00:33:09.382 --> 00:33:15.146
They succeeded the first time they tried
it, most of them never did it again.

00:33:15.146 --> 00:33:20.805
A few did, generally with less
success than they did the first time,

00:33:20.805 --> 00:33:27.943
but it is very few successful, successive
entrepreneurs of CEOs of multiple things.

00:33:27.943 --> 00:33:31.175
I say experience counts, but
the best succeed the first time.

00:33:31.175 --> 00:33:34.161
Here's where I say, and
this is contrarian thinking,

00:33:34.161 --> 00:33:37.464
and it's contrary into the experience
most of you will have,

00:33:37.464 --> 00:33:42.059
or you'll have a lot of jobs, you'll work
for a number of companies in your tenures.

00:33:42.059 --> 00:33:46.813
Maybe fairly short, and they're getting
shorter in this day and age, but

00:33:46.813 --> 00:33:51.791
I urge you each time that you think about
taking a job, whether it's the one you

00:33:51.791 --> 00:33:56.569
get out of here, when you hit that
reset button, or each subsequent time.

00:33:56.569 --> 00:34:00.514
I urge you to at least put some of your
effort into the thought process that this

00:34:00.514 --> 00:34:02.760
is the last change I'm ever going to make,
and

00:34:02.760 --> 00:34:06.179
I'm going to work for this company for
the rest of my working career.

00:34:06.179 --> 00:34:08.471
I'm going to run it one day, and

00:34:08.471 --> 00:34:13.524
then I'm going to retire from it
having spent my whole career here.

00:34:13.524 --> 00:34:16.605
Chances are pretty good it won't happen,
but

00:34:16.605 --> 00:34:19.851
that mindset will serve
you well no matter what.

00:34:19.851 --> 00:34:22.752
It happened to me, wasn't my first time,
wasn't my second time.

00:34:22.752 --> 00:34:26.504
I found something that I really liked,
I stayed with the company for 30 years,

00:34:26.504 --> 00:34:27.840
I ran it, I retired from it.

00:34:27.840 --> 00:34:33.559
So I admit my bias is characterized by my
personal experience which I freely admit.

00:34:33.559 --> 00:34:38.119
But don't go work for
a company that you don't respect,

00:34:38.119 --> 00:34:41.349
that you can't go home to your family and

00:34:41.349 --> 00:34:46.117
tell them what they do, and
have them feel good about that.

00:34:46.117 --> 00:34:49.312
It's not on the slide,
but don't go work for

00:34:49.312 --> 00:34:54.540
a company if you won't relocate to
where the corporate headquarters is.

00:34:54.540 --> 00:34:58.590
I can't tell you how many times
I've got this story, and it is so

00:34:58.590 --> 00:35:00.549
obvious that I have to tell it.

00:35:00.549 --> 00:35:04.400
I was interviewing a guy some years ago,

00:35:04.400 --> 00:35:10.639
who was a extremely successful
Regional Manager for Medtronic.

00:35:10.639 --> 00:35:13.931
Great company, wonderful company,
he was in Washington,

00:35:13.931 --> 00:35:15.680
DC running the eastern region.

00:35:15.680 --> 00:35:18.419
And they wanted him to
move to Minneapolis,

00:35:18.419 --> 00:35:21.389
which is where the company
was headquartered.

00:35:21.389 --> 00:35:27.439
And he didn't want to do that because
he want to stay in Washington,

00:35:27.439 --> 00:35:32.508
and I asked him, I said,
how is this not predictable?

00:35:32.508 --> 00:35:37.628
The only way that eventually you're not
going to get asked to go to the corporate

00:35:37.628 --> 00:35:43.242
headquarters is if a, the company fails or
gets sold, or b, you're not very good.

00:35:43.242 --> 00:35:47.232
And if you're not willing to
go where the seat of power is,

00:35:47.232 --> 00:35:52.035
why in the world that you spend the last
ten years working your ass off to

00:35:52.035 --> 00:35:56.530
generate this opportunity,
which now you're going to turn down?

00:35:56.530 --> 00:35:59.229
Explain to me how that makes any sense?

00:35:59.229 --> 00:36:02.121
So think about that.

00:36:02.121 --> 00:36:07.379
Run something hard,
my biases if you've got an opportunity

00:36:07.379 --> 00:36:11.706
to take a line job over a staff job,
do it, get P and

00:36:11.706 --> 00:36:16.569
L responsibility, get hire and
fire responsibility.

00:36:16.569 --> 00:36:20.571
Get something where you've got
budgets to run, people to hire,

00:36:20.571 --> 00:36:24.520
things to manage that can be
measurable if you're successful.

00:36:24.520 --> 00:36:29.365
And do the best to avoid things
that where your contribution isn't

00:36:29.365 --> 00:36:32.836
measurable even if
the company is successful.

00:36:32.836 --> 00:36:37.448
because you'll get some spin off from
being associated with a successful

00:36:37.448 --> 00:36:38.172
company.

00:36:38.172 --> 00:36:41.850
But it won't be as good as
if you can demonstrate that

00:36:41.850 --> 00:36:45.870
something good happened because
you let it or you did it.

00:36:45.870 --> 00:36:48.771
The people part,
sometimes people tend to think,

00:36:48.771 --> 00:36:51.409
I don't think anybody
here would think that.

00:36:51.409 --> 00:36:55.757
But sometimes people tend to think
that they don't want to hire really,

00:36:55.757 --> 00:36:59.091
really good people because
it'll make them look bad or

00:36:59.091 --> 00:37:02.654
they might overshadow them or
they might be risky to them.

00:37:02.654 --> 00:37:05.978
And I'll tell you that's
a prescription I think, for

00:37:05.978 --> 00:37:09.820
failure because two things,
you're not going to get promoted.

00:37:09.820 --> 00:37:13.480
This is if you're in a corporate job in
a big company, but pretty much anywhere.

00:37:13.480 --> 00:37:16.774
You're not going to get promoted to the
next big job unless whoever is going to

00:37:16.774 --> 00:37:20.069
promote you thinks there's somebody
in your organization that can take

00:37:20.069 --> 00:37:20.730
over for you.

00:37:20.730 --> 00:37:23.397
And if you haven't got anybody, well,

00:37:23.397 --> 00:37:27.711
you'll either sit in that job
until you do, or you'll get passed

00:37:27.711 --> 00:37:32.349
over because you are perceived as
having good people working for you.

00:37:32.349 --> 00:37:35.077
You work for great people,
they're going to get promoted,

00:37:35.077 --> 00:37:36.980
they're going to pull you up behind them.

00:37:36.980 --> 00:37:40.425
You hire really good people, they'll
push you up because they'll be ready for

00:37:40.425 --> 00:37:41.254
new challenges.

00:37:41.254 --> 00:37:44.743
You'll give it to them, that reflects
well on you, plus it makes you feel good.

00:37:44.743 --> 00:37:47.813
And the last thing is,

00:37:47.813 --> 00:37:52.829
there's nothing that I looked for.

00:37:52.829 --> 00:37:57.160
And when I scan resumes, and
I talked to people about their background,

00:37:57.160 --> 00:38:01.775
the first thing and the last thing, and
almost everything in-between that I

00:38:01.775 --> 00:38:05.410
looked at was, where and
when did this person get promoted?

00:38:05.410 --> 00:38:09.156
Because you get promoted when the people
or the company that you work for

00:38:09.156 --> 00:38:12.589
thought you did a good enough job,
and what the job they gave you,

00:38:12.589 --> 00:38:14.040
they give you a bigger one.

00:38:14.040 --> 00:38:17.731
And it's amazing,
when I looked at careers,

00:38:17.731 --> 00:38:24.264
we'd see backgrounds of somebody that
starts out, probably not a Stanford MBA,

00:38:24.264 --> 00:38:29.017
but they start out as a District
sales manager of Company A.

00:38:29.017 --> 00:38:32.639
And then a regional sales
leader of Company B, and

00:38:32.639 --> 00:38:36.270
then a sales and
marketing director of company C.

00:38:36.270 --> 00:38:37.236
And a VP of sales and

00:38:37.236 --> 00:38:40.628
marketing company E and
the chief operating officer of company F.

00:38:40.628 --> 00:38:46.630
Looks like a great progression of titles,
they never got promoted, never once.

00:38:46.630 --> 00:38:47.824
So it makes you wonder,

00:38:47.824 --> 00:38:50.948
were they just get out of town
just before the axe fell on them.

00:38:50.948 --> 00:38:55.602
And getting lucky to use their background
to get promoted to another company that

00:38:55.602 --> 00:38:58.417
would buy their story and
give them a bigger job.

00:38:58.417 --> 00:39:01.415
When you look around, you'll see
people like this all the time, and

00:39:01.415 --> 00:39:03.489
they're not the people
you want to go work for.

00:39:03.489 --> 00:39:06.328
For one thing, they're going to be
splitting, they're not going to

00:39:06.328 --> 00:39:09.329
take care of you because they're
only concerned about themselves.

00:39:09.329 --> 00:39:14.480
And you don't want to be one of
those people, so think about.

00:39:14.480 --> 00:39:18.886
If you're close, if you've got
an opportunity, or you're thinking about

00:39:18.886 --> 00:39:23.291
leaving and you're close to finishing
a project you can take credit for, and

00:39:23.291 --> 00:39:26.272
getting a promotion,
I would urge you think long and

00:39:26.272 --> 00:39:29.629
hard before you leave anywhere
just short of a promotion.

00:39:29.629 --> 00:39:31.813
Because somebody like
me is going to ask you,

00:39:31.813 --> 00:39:35.830
why did you leave just shorter when you
should have probably gotten promoted?

00:39:35.830 --> 00:39:38.742
Were are you going to get promoted?

00:39:38.742 --> 00:39:40.152
Why didn't you get promoted?

00:39:40.152 --> 00:39:43.381
Who got the job in that company
that you would have wanted?

00:39:43.381 --> 00:39:45.354
Were they better than you?

00:39:45.354 --> 00:39:48.993
So maybe I should be talking to them.

00:39:48.993 --> 00:39:51.659
So a few final thoughts and
we'll go to Q&amp;A.

00:39:51.659 --> 00:39:56.214
The world is big, your world is bigger
than most people's worlds because you have

00:39:56.214 --> 00:39:58.799
opportunities that most people don't have.

00:39:58.799 --> 00:40:02.176
You've earned them by being here,
by having the capabilities to get here,

00:40:02.176 --> 00:40:04.410
by making the sacrifice to come here.

00:40:04.410 --> 00:40:07.945
And so your opportunity set is large.

00:40:07.945 --> 00:40:12.530
Your networks, we talked on this, your
networks are much bigger than your biggest

00:40:12.530 --> 00:40:16.932
imagination of how big they can be,
they're bigger if you think expansively.

00:40:16.932 --> 00:40:18.561
People like to help.

00:40:18.561 --> 00:40:22.828
As I said before, make it easy for
people to help you.

00:40:22.828 --> 00:40:24.650
Don't make it hard, make it easy.

00:40:24.650 --> 00:40:27.308
They'll do it,
they'll do it over and over again.

00:40:27.308 --> 00:40:32.126
And be nice to them, somebody said, be
nice to the people you pass on your way up

00:40:32.126 --> 00:40:36.444
the corporate ladder, you may pass
them again on your way down, but-

00:40:36.444 --> 00:40:37.190
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:40:37.190 --> 00:40:39.680
&gt;&gt; Hope not, but you'll see them again.

00:40:41.160 --> 00:40:43.290
Success is measured in a lot of ways.

00:40:43.290 --> 00:40:46.398
We can talk about being the CEO
of a Fortune 1000 company, and

00:40:46.398 --> 00:40:49.520
maybe a couple of you in here will
do that, but most of you won't.

00:40:50.520 --> 00:40:52.900
And that doesn't mean
you're less successful.

00:40:52.900 --> 00:40:58.254
What matters is your success,
according to you, not according to me,

00:40:58.254 --> 00:41:02.016
or your classmates, or
the school, or surveys.

00:41:02.016 --> 00:41:08.770
What matters is your success according
to you, you measure it your way.

00:41:08.770 --> 00:41:13.135
And don't let anybody convince you that
you should measure your success in life

00:41:13.135 --> 00:41:17.060
any more than your happiness in
life by somebody else's standards.

00:41:17.060 --> 00:41:21.883
They've got their own issues,
they've got their own goals.

00:41:21.883 --> 00:41:23.971
You should have yours and

00:41:23.971 --> 00:41:29.450
you should have every reason to
expect that you can achieve them.

00:41:29.450 --> 00:41:34.300
Last comment is don't hit
the panic button too soon.

00:41:34.300 --> 00:41:35.130
Life is long.

00:41:35.130 --> 00:41:36.706
Enjoy the journey.

00:41:36.706 --> 00:41:39.120
You've got a lot of great
experiences along the way.

00:41:39.120 --> 00:41:43.530
There's a lot of people who will help
you and appreciate what you do, and

00:41:43.530 --> 00:41:46.480
I can't, I wish I was out there.

00:41:46.480 --> 00:41:48.688
And there's an Irish comedian
that used to say, gee,

00:41:48.688 --> 00:41:50.947
I wish I was out there in
the audience listening to this.

00:41:50.947 --> 00:41:53.464
But, in some ways,
I wish I was out there with

00:41:53.464 --> 00:41:57.987
the opportunities that you all have
because they're really almost limitless.

00:41:57.987 --> 00:42:02.036
And all I can say is I wish
you good luck with them and

00:42:02.036 --> 00:42:04.943
we'll be tracking your progress.

00:42:06.542 --> 00:42:12.277
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Hopefully

00:42:12.277 --> 00:42:18.189
there's something there that
gets some questions going, yeah.

00:42:18.189 --> 00:42:22.260
&gt;&gt; Can you talk a little bit about the
process of getting wrapped up into a job

00:42:22.260 --> 00:42:26.200
search with a recruiter, starting
with how you identify those four or

00:42:26.200 --> 00:42:28.561
five out of the 500 that are relevant.

00:42:32.087 --> 00:42:37.313
&gt;&gt; Yeah the question is, how do you
identify the recruiters who are right for

00:42:37.313 --> 00:42:40.948
you to begin making
a relationship with over time.

00:42:43.377 --> 00:42:46.123
There's two or three ways,
and I would try them all.

00:42:47.350 --> 00:42:51.170
It starts by figuring out what
it is that you want to do.

00:42:51.170 --> 00:42:54.294
And being as clear and
specific about that as you can, and

00:42:54.294 --> 00:42:57.030
then you get to people
in the recruiting firms.

00:42:57.030 --> 00:43:00.932
And there's five large ones, large,
large global ones, mine was one.

00:43:00.932 --> 00:43:05.179
And then there's a dozen or so mid
tiered ones and then lots of small ones,

00:43:05.179 --> 00:43:08.620
many of whom are founded and
staffed by alums of the big one.

00:43:08.620 --> 00:43:12.150
So the quality is just as good,
they may be small.

00:43:12.150 --> 00:43:15.608
You start asking people around
who does this kind of work and

00:43:15.608 --> 00:43:18.870
you ask enough questions,
the same names will come up.

00:43:19.920 --> 00:43:25.960
Second way to do it is reverse
engineer searches that got done.

00:43:25.960 --> 00:43:30.909
If there's a job out there that
you would like to have had or

00:43:30.909 --> 00:43:36.110
would like to have the next time,
and it just got filled.

00:43:36.110 --> 00:43:37.826
You saw the announcement in the paper,

00:43:37.826 --> 00:43:40.272
chances are there was a recruiter
that did that search.

00:43:40.272 --> 00:43:43.675
Call up the company, call up
the individual in the job and say, look,

00:43:43.675 --> 00:43:46.055
I see you got this promotion or
send him an email.

00:43:46.055 --> 00:43:47.738
You can reach him on LinkedIn by the way.

00:43:47.738 --> 00:43:49.410
We'll come back to that.

00:43:49.410 --> 00:43:53.743
And say, I'm curious as to was there
a recruiter that handled this search and

00:43:53.743 --> 00:43:56.480
if so, who was it and were they good?

00:43:56.480 --> 00:43:58.710
Because if they were good for
them, they'll be good for you.

00:43:58.710 --> 00:44:02.556
The one thing about the recruiting
profession is once you start building

00:44:02.556 --> 00:44:05.405
the reputation for
doing certain kinds of searches.

00:44:05.405 --> 00:44:09.595
The industry knows and they'll come back
to the same people time and time again for

00:44:09.595 --> 00:44:10.513
those projects.

00:44:10.513 --> 00:44:13.806
That's how they build specializations and

00:44:13.806 --> 00:44:17.460
the same thing happens
within the search firms.

00:44:18.680 --> 00:44:22.567
I got a lot of calls because I was
high profile from companies wanting

00:44:22.567 --> 00:44:23.530
searches done.

00:44:24.810 --> 00:44:26.690
Almost never would I do them.

00:44:26.690 --> 00:44:30.110
So the question would be,
who would we recommend that they talk to?

00:44:30.110 --> 00:44:33.811
And it's going to be the person in our
firm that has the best track record doing

00:44:33.811 --> 00:44:37.660
those kinds of searches and
everybody knows who they are.

00:44:37.660 --> 00:44:42.420
And so, at that point, your goal is
to figure out who those people are.

00:44:42.420 --> 00:44:47.831
And try to get an opportunity
to have them know who you are.

00:44:47.831 --> 00:44:53.635
And if they're willing to spend a little
bit of time, not a lot of time, but 15,

00:44:53.635 --> 00:44:59.441
20 minutes meeting you, and just saying,
yeah, maybe I'm not a candidate for

00:44:59.441 --> 00:45:04.597
anything right now, but I'm a candidate
for what you do as a recruiter.

00:45:04.597 --> 00:45:08.197
And one of these days you're going to
have an assignment that I'm going to be

00:45:08.197 --> 00:45:11.500
qualified for and interested in and
I wanted you to know who I was.

00:45:12.580 --> 00:45:16.699
And that line of discussion
works if you're right,

00:45:16.699 --> 00:45:21.298
if your facts are right, and
you've done your homework.

00:45:21.298 --> 00:45:28.188
It's a waste of time if it's a random
blast at the recruiting world.

00:45:28.188 --> 00:45:32.882
And just as it's pretty much a waste of
time to send resumes to recruiting firms,

00:45:32.882 --> 00:45:37.384
don't bother, it's not worth the paper
you're printing your resumes on.

00:45:37.384 --> 00:45:42.683
