more likely have worked for them, or for

01:01:23.864 --> 01:01:25.845
their other teams over the years.

01:01:25.845 --> 01:01:30.568
And really good executives, I find tend to
take very good care of people who perform

01:01:30.568 --> 01:01:31.990
for them over the years.

01:01:31.990 --> 01:01:34.294
I mean, they'll recommend them for jobs,

01:01:34.294 --> 01:01:37.090
they'll proactively recommend them for
jobs.

01:01:37.090 --> 01:01:39.418
And in fact, if you've got
a good relationship with them,

01:01:39.418 --> 01:01:41.080
you can go ask them to do that.

01:01:41.080 --> 01:01:43.310
Go to somebody who is a boss
of yours in another company.

01:01:43.310 --> 01:01:47.701
If that was a good relationship,
he or she has a good feeling for you,

01:01:47.701 --> 01:01:51.390
call them and say, look,
I'm ready to make a move.

01:01:51.390 --> 01:01:52.750
Who should I be talking to?

01:01:52.750 --> 01:01:58.645
Who in this search firm world, do you know
that you'd be willing to introduce me to,

01:01:58.645 --> 01:02:02.813
and if somebody reaches out to you,
well, keep me in mind.

01:02:02.813 --> 01:02:07.758
So, Okay
&gt;&gt; How do I translate?

01:02:07.758 --> 01:02:12.440
&gt;&gt; Well, two things.

01:02:12.440 --> 01:02:17.580
One, if they're international firms,
that have offices in the UK, and you have

01:02:17.580 --> 01:02:22.647
good relationships with the recruiters
in the UK, ask them if they'd be willing

01:02:22.647 --> 01:02:27.584
to introduce you to their counterparts
here in Silicon valley in their firms.

01:02:27.584 --> 01:02:31.693
Most of them will be willing to do
that if the firms are large and

01:02:31.693 --> 01:02:33.680
have offices in both places.

01:02:37.110 --> 01:02:43.266
If not, then the other source is,
it could be the UK expat community here,

01:02:43.266 --> 01:02:47.590
people who are Brits
are working in Silicon Valley.

01:02:47.590 --> 01:02:48.460
There's lots of them.

01:02:48.460 --> 01:02:49.922
And my experience has been.

01:02:49.922 --> 01:02:53.685
As I said before,
they're willing to help their countrymen,

01:02:53.685 --> 01:02:58.360
because they know you're in a strange
land here and you need a little help.

01:02:58.360 --> 01:03:03.339
So those would probably be my
two first places that I would go

01:03:03.339 --> 01:03:08.630
beyond just the normal networking
that I talked about before.

01:03:08.630 --> 01:03:10.480
I will take you, and then you, yeah.

01:03:10.480 --> 01:03:13.130
&gt;&gt; I'm a little bit reticent
to ask a question up here.

01:03:13.130 --> 01:03:15.872
So I'm afraid that you can read minds.

01:03:15.872 --> 01:03:16.446
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

01:03:16.446 --> 01:03:19.750
&gt;&gt; Anyway, so let me
&gt;&gt; have a good day sometimes.

01:03:19.750 --> 01:03:24.138
So on the topic of magical powers, if you
could look into the future, say 20 or

01:03:24.138 --> 01:03:28.923
30 years ahead, hypothetically, a lot of
CEOs previously came from operations and

01:03:28.923 --> 01:03:30.780
then sales and then the finance.

01:03:32.070 --> 01:03:35.625
Will there be a chain,
the origin of where CEOs come from

01:03:35.625 --> 01:03:40.482
particularly if they've got a technology
background are technologists.

01:03:40.482 --> 01:03:46.200
And we got to be getting that we got to
have the case to the executive washroom.

01:03:46.200 --> 01:03:51.349
&gt;&gt; Well, some do, and some have I think

01:03:55.409 --> 01:03:59.760
Technologists are going to continue
to run technology companies.

01:04:00.930 --> 01:04:04.286
And if you look at even
the near term future,

01:04:04.286 --> 01:04:09.553
most of the important companies
are going to be technology companies,

01:04:09.553 --> 01:04:13.818
or are going to be trying to
claim to the world that they're

01:04:13.818 --> 01:04:17.850
technology companies even
if they're really not.

01:04:19.680 --> 01:04:24.720
I don't know if you saw the cover
article in Fortune this weekend,

01:04:24.720 --> 01:04:30.660
a picture of Bob Iger of Disney on the
cover talking about Disney as a technology

01:04:30.660 --> 01:04:35.713
company and his efforts to remake
Disney as a technology company.

01:04:35.713 --> 01:04:43.119
So Bob had no technology in his
background and he's now a technical guru.

01:04:46.472 --> 01:04:51.474
I still think as I mentioned before,
that the people that are going to

01:04:51.474 --> 01:04:55.762
move up are the ones that have
taken responsibilities for

01:04:55.762 --> 01:04:59.711
pieces of business and
have run them successfully.

01:04:59.711 --> 01:05:05.027
And you can get a business unit in
a larger company or even a smaller

01:05:05.027 --> 01:05:10.456
company to run if you set out to do
that pretty early in your career.

01:05:10.456 --> 01:05:13.908
It might not be a massive one but
if you take a small one and

01:05:13.908 --> 01:05:18.994
turn it into a medium sized one, that's
going to be, that's a pretty good record.

01:05:18.994 --> 01:05:21.262
Then the next time around,
they give you a medium sized one,

01:05:21.262 --> 01:05:24.178
you turn it into a big one, you're
successful at that, you can do anything.

01:05:24.178 --> 01:05:28.647
And so I actually think the question,

01:05:28.647 --> 01:05:35.570
I'd almost come back at that question and
reverse it.

01:05:35.570 --> 01:05:40.199
And it's not so
much what functional areas they're

01:05:40.199 --> 01:05:44.100
going to be looking to for
their next leaders.

01:05:46.100 --> 01:05:49.020
They're going to look for leaders.

01:05:49.020 --> 01:05:52.067
And they're going to take them
from wherever they can find them,

01:05:52.067 --> 01:05:53.620
whatever they've been doing,

01:05:53.620 --> 01:05:57.245
if they think they have the leadership
capabilities to run their company or

01:05:57.245 --> 01:06:00.888
if we think they have the leadership
capabilities to run another company.

01:06:02.432 --> 01:06:07.820
But you gotta be in a position to have
shown your leadership capabilities

01:06:07.820 --> 01:06:13.028
before somebody can look at you and
say, this is a leader who we can give

01:06:13.028 --> 01:06:17.914
a bigger job to and if it's been
all staff, that's really hard.

01:06:17.914 --> 01:06:22.156
Some combination of line and staff if
you've got credible line experience and

01:06:22.156 --> 01:06:26.024
some great executives have gone back and
forth between line and staff.

01:06:26.024 --> 01:06:30.939
And there's a few that have gotten
to the top of big companies,

01:06:30.939 --> 01:06:35.616
all staff through consulting or,
Staff jobs,

01:06:35.616 --> 01:06:43.812
typically more finance than say the softer
ones like planning or HR or PR.

01:06:43.812 --> 01:06:48.967
But I still think that setting
out to build a record of

01:06:48.967 --> 01:06:56.834
having been successful running something
where that success can be measured.

01:06:56.834 --> 01:07:04.360
And people recognize it is the best
path that I can find to leadership.

01:07:04.360 --> 01:07:09.787
There's a question I wasn't asked yet,
at least today but I'm going to ask it and

01:07:09.787 --> 01:07:15.141
then answer it because it gets back to
setting the reset button in your career.

01:07:15.141 --> 01:07:19.923
And some of you here may want to go and
make a radical shift.

01:07:19.923 --> 01:07:25.243
I don't know that to be true,
but there's always some and

01:07:25.243 --> 01:07:31.216
if you've been doing consumer products and
you want to go to tech,

01:07:31.216 --> 01:07:35.908
or even all big company and
you want to go to startup.

01:07:35.908 --> 01:07:41.031
And people ask me about, How that kind of

01:07:41.031 --> 01:07:46.800
a desire matches up with
executive recruiters.

01:07:46.800 --> 01:07:48.735
And the answer is, it doesn't.

01:07:48.735 --> 01:07:50.930
Hardly at all.

01:07:50.930 --> 01:07:55.794
And you will frustrate
yourself a lot if in fact

01:07:55.794 --> 01:08:00.536
you're trying to make that work because 99

01:08:00.536 --> 01:08:04.916
times out of 100 it isn't going to work.

01:08:04.916 --> 01:08:11.430
What recruiters want, they want an easy
path to a solution of a client problem.

01:08:11.430 --> 01:08:13.780
And they've got a defined spec.

01:08:13.780 --> 01:08:19.060
And let's call it a marketing director

01:08:19.060 --> 01:08:23.383
position for PC apps business.

01:08:23.383 --> 01:08:26.248
Well who are they going to present
as candidates on that search?

01:08:26.248 --> 01:08:31.568
They're going to present probably
three candidates after a work.

01:08:31.568 --> 01:08:37.246
They want to present the top individual
in that job for their client company's

01:08:37.246 --> 01:08:42.591
biggest competitor, or the number two
person in a company a lot bigger.

01:08:44.092 --> 01:08:48.555
Or somebody two levels
up In a smaller company

01:08:48.555 --> 01:08:53.026
in the same space or
a closely related space.

01:08:53.026 --> 01:08:56.962
So, basically somebody that's
done this job before and

01:08:56.962 --> 01:09:00.412
can come in here and
do this job for my client fast.

01:09:00.412 --> 01:09:05.042
They're not going to present
somebody who was a finance manager

01:09:05.042 --> 01:09:09.405
in a consumer project products
company that got his MBA and

01:09:09.405 --> 01:09:12.713
now wants to be a marketing
manager in tech.

01:09:12.713 --> 01:09:15.597
It's not going to happen,

01:09:15.597 --> 01:09:20.962
that job can happen but
not down that channel.

01:09:20.962 --> 01:09:26.224
If you want to make a jump shift,
what is what we call a jump shift,

01:09:26.224 --> 01:09:29.839
change of function and change of industry.

01:09:31.461 --> 01:09:36.451
You're likely not going to get much
help from the executive recruiting

01:09:36.451 --> 01:09:38.178
profession to do that.

01:09:38.178 --> 01:09:39.310
And that's just a fact.

01:09:39.310 --> 01:09:41.837
And I just want to tell
you that clear because

01:09:41.837 --> 01:09:47.360
don't waste your time trying to convince
them to do that because they're not.

01:09:47.360 --> 01:09:49.080
You gotta go find that another way.

01:09:49.080 --> 01:09:53.614
And the way you find it is typically by
somebody who knows you, that's seen you

01:09:53.614 --> 01:09:58.353
work somewhere else before and is in a
position to say, yeah, I know you've never

01:09:58.353 --> 01:10:02.640
done that before but I know you can do
it because I've seen what you can do.

01:10:03.650 --> 01:10:08.390
That's not how that get, that doesn't get
done through a third party recruiter,

01:10:08.390 --> 01:10:11.844
but it gets done every day through
your personal networks and

01:10:11.844 --> 01:10:13.730
your personal contacts.

01:10:13.730 --> 01:10:17.460
So, if that's what you want to do,
then that's what you gotta work.

01:10:17.460 --> 01:10:21.050
If you know what you want to do and
you've done it and

01:10:21.050 --> 01:10:24.386
you've got demonstrable
record of doing it,

01:10:24.386 --> 01:10:29.430
then there are recruiters out there
that are going to jump all over you and

01:10:29.430 --> 01:10:35.090
try to get you your next job in that
field, either a jump or a shift.

01:10:35.090 --> 01:10:37.883
See, by a jump, higher level job,

01:10:37.883 --> 01:10:43.954
doing the same thing up from director
to VP, same industry, same function.

01:10:43.954 --> 01:10:47.247
Shift, same function, different industry,

01:10:47.247 --> 01:10:51.131
Director of Marketing from
consumer products to tech.

01:10:51.131 --> 01:10:55.120
Yeah, probably if there's some
credible way you can do it.

01:10:55.120 --> 01:10:57.040
So I call that a jump or a shift.

01:10:57.040 --> 01:11:00.411
But if you try to do what in basketball or
some they call the jump shift,

01:11:00.411 --> 01:11:03.383
which is change of level and
change of industry and function,

01:11:03.383 --> 01:11:07.030
change your function and
change your industry, very very hard.

01:11:07.030 --> 01:11:09.512
And you gotta go work that on your own.

01:11:10.698 --> 01:11:12.272
Yes, thank you.

01:11:12.272 --> 01:11:15.879
&gt;&gt; That was my question but
I have another one.

01:11:15.879 --> 01:11:20.237
Have you also to recruiters and to future
employees promote the year that you

01:11:20.237 --> 01:11:23.029
took here,
because I've got mixed feedback and

01:11:23.029 --> 01:11:26.320
then were like,
why have you already had a great career?

01:11:26.320 --> 01:11:27.940
Why would you break that?

01:11:27.940 --> 01:11:32.025
And go and take an exam,
like a one year program and send forth.

01:11:32.025 --> 01:11:34.534
What is your best answer
to that apart from

01:11:34.534 --> 01:11:37.978
obviously the personal
&gt;&gt; What's your best

01:11:37.978 --> 01:11:39.972
answer to that question?

01:11:39.972 --> 01:11:42.853
&gt;&gt; That I wanted to,
I have the headquarter, sorry,

01:11:42.853 --> 01:11:45.333
I didn't want to stay with my headquarter.

01:11:45.333 --> 01:11:47.731
So I wanted to have a career break.

01:11:47.731 --> 01:11:50.002
I wanted to switch not my function but

01:11:50.002 --> 01:11:54.994
unfortunately my company because I didn't
want to be with their headquarters.

01:11:54.994 --> 01:11:58.024
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; And I wanted to use that time To really

01:11:58.024 --> 01:12:02.420
get some educational aspects and
a new perspective.

01:12:02.420 --> 01:12:06.320
&gt;&gt; Okay, I'd say that, sorry, it's fair.

01:12:06.320 --> 01:12:08.860
I think you can improve that.

01:12:08.860 --> 01:12:14.360
And I think you would improve
it by being more positive

01:12:14.360 --> 01:12:19.005
about the opportunity that
you want to go to and

01:12:19.005 --> 01:12:24.262
less negative about the one
that you were leaving,

01:12:24.262 --> 01:12:28.678
because nobody really wants to hear that.

01:12:28.678 --> 01:12:32.847
I mean, for example, you had learned all
that you could learn, you knew you were

01:12:32.847 --> 01:12:36.648
missing some skills to move up to
the next critical level in management and

01:12:36.648 --> 01:12:40.328
you thought this was the most time
efficient way that you could add those

01:12:40.328 --> 01:12:44.005
skills and at the same time enhance
your career and move to a bigger job in

01:12:44.005 --> 01:12:48.770
a different company because you didn't
see that opportunity where you were.

01:12:48.770 --> 01:12:53.182
To me that's a much more positive story,
some version of that.

01:12:53.182 --> 01:12:58.103
Again, I think nobody's
going to believe the story

01:12:58.103 --> 01:13:02.480
that you tell them unless you believe it.

01:13:02.480 --> 01:13:07.679
So, first off, you have to believe it,
you have to be convincing in telling it,

01:13:07.679 --> 01:13:12.120
but to the extent that it can be much
more positive and less negative.

01:13:12.120 --> 01:13:14.870
This is your elevator pitch and

01:13:14.870 --> 01:13:20.157
sort of like what you'd tell
somebody if they say you got two

01:13:20.157 --> 01:13:26.480
minutes to tell me why I should
give you another ten minutes.

01:13:26.480 --> 01:13:30.363
You want to have a really good story
that you can tell when you do that,.

01:13:30.363 --> 01:13:33.900
And I would just say,
don't be defensive, no reason to.

01:13:33.900 --> 01:13:38.507
There's not a reason in the world for
any of you to be defensive coming here.

01:13:38.507 --> 01:13:41.930
You've made a big
investment of your life and

01:13:41.930 --> 01:13:46.535
your time to improve your
skills dramatically, I believe.

01:13:46.535 --> 01:13:51.552
And I think most people would assume that.

01:13:51.552 --> 01:13:55.701
There are some MBA haters in the world,
some of them are vocal,

01:13:55.701 --> 01:14:00.040
some of them are active in
the venture capital community.

01:14:00.040 --> 01:14:03.108
Some of them have gone out of their way
to say that an MBA is a complete waste of

01:14:03.108 --> 01:14:04.240
time and money.

01:14:04.240 --> 01:14:07.464
In fact, a college education is
a complete waste of time and money,

01:14:07.464 --> 01:14:09.410
you should just go code.

01:14:09.410 --> 01:14:15.617
Well, easy for
them to say because you're billionaires.

01:14:15.617 --> 01:14:20.304
But you probably you're not going to get
very far convincing one of those people

01:14:20.304 --> 01:14:23.320
that your time here was well spent.

01:14:23.320 --> 01:14:26.158
So I go back to the Jim Collins
analogy of the open door,

01:14:26.158 --> 01:14:30.298
forget about those people and go spend
your time with people who appreciate what

01:14:30.298 --> 01:14:33.250
you did here, and there's lots of people.

01:14:33.250 --> 01:14:37.906
And you don't even have to take an
editorial position about who's right and

01:14:37.906 --> 01:14:39.430
who's wrong.

01:14:39.430 --> 01:14:44.505
But I can tell you clearly one of them is
a much more likely opportunity set for

01:14:44.505 --> 01:14:49.520
you than the other one without whether
you agree with their premise or not.

01:14:50.730 --> 01:14:53.406
If they think this is a waste of
time they're not going to help you,

01:14:53.406 --> 01:14:54.260
go somewhere else.

01:14:55.310 --> 01:14:59.441
Yeah, okay.

01:14:59.441 --> 01:15:04.610
&gt;&gt; I'm curious about what we'd like to
say about compensation to get some words.

01:15:04.610 --> 01:15:05.430
&gt;&gt; About compensation?

01:15:07.270 --> 01:15:13.970
Let me see if I can boil it down
into a couple of key points.

01:15:18.180 --> 01:15:21.450
One is, ask a lot of questions.

01:15:22.470 --> 01:15:28.020
Because the more information you have,
the more power that you will have.

01:15:29.170 --> 01:15:36.684
Compensation has a lot more elements to
it typically than most people identify.

01:15:36.684 --> 01:15:42.662
At senior levels, a compensation package
is going to have maybe as many as eight or

01:15:42.662 --> 01:15:45.330
ten major components.

01:15:45.330 --> 01:15:51.072
We tend to think about base salary,
cash incentive bonus and

01:15:51.072 --> 01:15:55.126
equity grants, kind of the big three, but

01:15:55.126 --> 01:16:00.758
there's relocation allowances,
home buying programs,

01:16:00.758 --> 01:16:04.702
pension gross-ups, signing bonuses,

01:16:04.702 --> 01:16:10.224
special project-based incentive
incentive options and

01:16:10.224 --> 01:16:14.160
as many things as people can dream up.

01:16:14.160 --> 01:16:18.753
So the first thing you want to do when
you're in a compensation discussion with

01:16:18.753 --> 01:16:23.960
anybody is find out how many of those
elements might possibly come into play.

01:16:23.960 --> 01:16:26.590
Do you have project-based bonuses?

01:16:26.590 --> 01:16:29.220
Do you use signing bonuses?

01:16:29.220 --> 01:16:33.792
How do you reward option
grants if I'm relocating?

01:16:33.792 --> 01:16:35.421
How do you deal with relocation?

01:16:35.421 --> 01:16:37.522
Is a home buyout part of that blah,
blah, blah?

01:16:37.522 --> 01:16:43.006
And so now you've got
a framework of what's possible.

01:16:43.006 --> 01:16:47.068
And there might be something on
that list that could be really,

01:16:47.068 --> 01:16:48.687
really valuable to you.

01:16:48.687 --> 01:16:54.790
If you're going to work for Stanford,
your kids get to get to go to Stanford.

01:16:54.790 --> 01:16:57.117
I mean, if you get college age kids,
I mean,

01:16:57.117 --> 01:17:00.570
I know people who have stayed here
because of that, that's huge.

01:17:00.570 --> 01:17:04.340
And so find out what the list is.

01:17:04.340 --> 01:17:09.301
And then the other thing I tell people,
a compensation discussion is a little

01:17:09.301 --> 01:17:12.674
bit like working at a mixing
board in a sound studio.

01:17:12.674 --> 01:17:15.890
You ever see these things, they got all
these, they're trying to manage the sound.

01:17:15.890 --> 01:17:18.446
Probably there's a guy back there
behind the wall doing that right now.

01:17:18.446 --> 01:17:22.914
And you got about ten levers that,
various levels of the sound,

01:17:22.914 --> 01:17:27.840
you can't push them all to maximum
because you just get garbage.

01:17:27.840 --> 01:17:29.650
It's the same thing in
a compensation discussion.

01:17:29.650 --> 01:17:33.510
You can't push everything to maximum but
you can probably push something.

01:17:34.510 --> 01:17:37.300
And one of the things that I tell people,

01:17:37.300 --> 01:17:40.580
try to find the soft
money if there is any.

01:17:40.580 --> 01:17:45.912
I mean, maybe the individual you're
talking to has a severe salary cap and

01:17:45.912 --> 01:17:48.281
they just can't go any higher but

01:17:48.281 --> 01:17:52.620
they've got a lot of
discretion on a signing bonus.

01:17:52.620 --> 01:17:54.750
Well, money's money, okay?

01:17:54.750 --> 01:17:58.897
So you don't get, and so
maybe you don't get that extra 25 or

01:17:58.897 --> 01:18:03.916
$50,000 in salary but
you pick up $100,000 signing bonus.

01:18:03.916 --> 01:18:08.824
Well, that's pretty good because you're
going to have a salary negotiation

01:18:08.824 --> 01:18:12.992
again in a year but maybe you can
negotiate it to six months and

01:18:12.992 --> 01:18:15.240
get a review in six months.

01:18:15.240 --> 01:18:16.820
Again, what's the review cycle?

01:18:16.820 --> 01:18:23.128
That's one of the items on the list
oeople don't tend to think about.

01:18:23.128 --> 01:18:25.887
So, recognize at the end
of the day that if you

01:18:25.887 --> 01:18:29.780
push too many of the levers
you could blow the deal.

01:18:29.780 --> 01:18:32.644
But you can push some and
people expect you to push some.

01:18:32.644 --> 01:18:38.075
They're I think particularly
impressed if you do it intelligently,

01:18:38.075 --> 01:18:43.421
particularly if you push something
that's not one of the top three.

01:18:43.421 --> 01:18:46.670
And if you've done your homework,
first off, I mean at a minimum,

01:18:46.670 --> 01:18:50.204
if you're replacing somebody,
you should be able to get everything that

01:18:50.204 --> 01:18:54.060
that individual got because that's
already been approved in the system.

01:18:54.060 --> 01:18:55.680
So that's a starting point.

01:18:55.680 --> 01:19:00.722
And usually if you ask,
you can find out what that is.

01:19:00.722 --> 01:19:03.543
If you know what industry counterparts
that their competitors get for

01:19:03.543 --> 01:19:06.350
the same job, that's useful information.

01:19:06.350 --> 01:19:09.207
And so, again, do your homework,

01:19:09.207 --> 01:19:14.449
figure out what the elements are,
push the one that in particular

01:19:14.449 --> 01:19:19.234
is worth more to you to get than
it is to them to give it to you.

01:19:19.234 --> 01:19:23.440
And that's where you can really, if you
push on something where they're resisting,

01:19:23.440 --> 01:19:25.000
that's going to be hard.

01:19:25.000 --> 01:19:27.219
So go find somewhere else to.

01:19:27.219 --> 01:19:30.925
So it's another version of an open door
[LAUGH] but it's go find something

01:19:30.925 --> 01:19:34.950
that's easy as opposed to fighting
over something that's hard.

01:19:34.950 --> 01:19:37.950
And, I mean, at the end of the day,

01:19:37.950 --> 01:19:43.050
I always try to encourage people
in that negotiation to try to

01:19:43.050 --> 01:19:49.490
get the company to accept that you
want to bet on your own performance.

01:19:49.490 --> 01:19:56.090
So go for bigger upside on bonuses,
shorter reviews, project bonuses.

01:19:56.090 --> 01:19:58.293
Okay, I'll go in at this level,

01:19:58.293 --> 01:20:01.927
it's a little less than what
I want in terms of salary.

01:20:01.927 --> 01:20:06.648
But given that, is it possible that
I can have a review of six months and

01:20:06.648 --> 01:20:10.180
if the performance warrants
I get that adjustment?

01:20:12.010 --> 01:20:12.940
That's pretty soft.

01:20:12.940 --> 01:20:13.763
They're not given much.

01:20:13.763 --> 01:20:15.410
They're not giving away any money.

01:20:15.410 --> 01:20:17.520
They may well agree to that.

01:20:17.520 --> 01:20:20.720
And if you perform,
then you get it in six months.

01:20:20.720 --> 01:20:24.721
If you don't perform,
you weren't going to get it anyway.

01:20:24.721 --> 01:20:27.707
[MUSIC]

